  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 3: A real equalizer, budgeted for the wrong channel"
            prompt="Pre-emphasize the transmitter for the channel - taps are hand-set (TX mode on, auto-adapt off, the way you'd dial in a first guess before ever closing a training loop) using the 11 dB the datasheet claims, the way you would if you trusted the paperwork instead of Bring-Up 2's measurement."
            hint="The FFE's stem plot will show real, sensible pre-emphasis - it's not flat, and it's not backwards. Compare its boost at Nyquist (under the plot) to the channel's actual measured loss, not to what the datasheet said the channel should be."
            explanation="These taps are exactly the ones that converge cleanly for an 11 dB channel - a real, correctly-shaped equalizer (right direction, sensible magnitudes), just budgeted for the wrong number. Against the real 16 dB channel from Bring-Up 2, the eye only reaches about 39% height - clearly short of the roughly 55% you get from the same FFE re-budgeted for the real measured loss (a bit more post-cursor tap, a bit less main tap, to spend more of the budget cancelling the extra 5 dB). This is exactly the bring-up trap Bring-Up 2 existed to prevent: an equalizer that looks completely reasonable - correct shape, correct direction, real non-zero taps - but calibrated off a datasheet number instead of what you actually measured."/>
</LABCHAIN>
